<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Old Testament</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as OT</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Old Testament is the first division of the Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, based primarily upon the 24 books of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, or Tanakh, a collection of religious writings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and occasionally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>. It differs from the Jewish canon in order, textual tradition and, in some traditions, contents: the Catholic canon has 46 books, the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oriental-orthodox-churches">Oriental Orthodox Churches</a> up to 49 and the Protestant Bible typically 39. Christians traditionally divide it into the Pentateuch, the Historical books, the Poetic Books and the Prophetic books. Books in the Christian Old Testament but not in the Hebrew canon, mostly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a>, are called deuterocanonical.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/old-testament.jpg" alt="Old Testament" width="330" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rembrandt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rembrandt_Harmensz._van_Rijn_079.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Old Testament</b> is the Hebrew Bible, which Christians read as pointing to Christ; Catholic and Orthodox Bibles include further books, from the Greek Septuagint, that Protestants call the Apocrypha.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hebrew Bible, as the Christian <b>Old Testament</b>, became the scripture of half the world, and its ideas of one God, of a moral law binding on all, of the dignity of every human being made in God’s image and of history moving toward redemption shaped the civilisations…</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its fruit was an Ethiopic Bible of 81 books, 46 in the <b>Old Testament</b> and 35 in the New, among them Jubilees, the Ascension of Isaiah and the Book of Enoch.</blockquote><a href="/language/geez">Ge'ez · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The largest part comes from the Bible of Ulfilas, of which roughly three-quarters of the New Testament and some fragments of the <b>Old Testament</b> survive, in codices mostly of the 6th century, long after he died.</blockquote><a href="/language/gothic">Gothic · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Hebrew is the language of the Hebrew Bible, one of the most influential texts in human history, sacred to Jews, and to Christians as the <b>Old Testament</b>, and revered in Islam.</blockquote><a href="/language/hebrew">Hebrew · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Robert F. Yeager notes that its only biblical references are to the <b>Old Testament</b>, that Christ is never mentioned and that its characters are pagans.</blockquote><a href="/work/beowulf">Beowulf · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
